  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "FULL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 5.0,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.95,
    "explanation": "Both of you: the 5.00 SGD no-show fee charged on trip TRP-05057 is refunded in full.\n\nWas the driver at the pickup? The trip record (EV-TRIP-05057) shows the driver pressed 'arrived' at 08:06:50 and computes arrived_by_deadline = true, and the driver's advocate asks us to take that at face value. It cannot be taken that way. The Tribunal's arrival test (POL-NS-21, category arrival_test) provides that the driver is at the pickup only when the trip record's arrival event is corroborated by the GPS trace within the arrival radius — 50 m for this STANDARD-class pickup. On the driver-device trace (EV-GPS-05057, COMPLETE, weight 1.0: 47 fixes, 100% coverage, no gaps, max gap 30 s against the 120 s impairment threshold, every fix reporting 3–9 m accuracy and so passing the 25 m filter), the nearest fix inside the arrival window is 476.59 m from the marked pickup, and from 08:06:50 to 08:18:50 the driver sits stationary roughly 560–650 m from the pickup coordinate. The arrival event is not corroborated. A tap records only that it was tapped; it does not establish the fact. Independently of the trace, the driver's own first chat message (EV-CHAT-05057, MSG-05057-001, 08:07:10) reads 'I reach already at Blk 215 carpark' while the booked pickup is Block 251, Hougang Avenue 3 — a contemporaneous admission against his own interest, which would support the finding even with the GPS set aside. The driver's answer, that the 50 m radius is 'merely an evidence procedure' that voids nothing, is rejected: it is the procedure that decides whether the driver was at the pickup, which is a fact POL-NS-24 and POL-NS-21 both need.\n\nWhat follows. Under POL-NS-24, if the driver fails to arrive at the pickup by the scheduled pickup time plus 5 minutes (08:10:00 + 300 s = 08:15:00, derived driver_deadline), any fee charged is refunded in full. The driver had not arrived at the pickup by that deadline or at all, so the rider's ground is made out and the 5.00 SGD on the payments ledger (EV-FARE-05057: fee_charged_sgd 5.00, refund_issued_sgd 0.00, fee_matches_policy true) is returned. The same conclusion follows on the driver's own free-wait argument: the free wait under POL-NS-21 is time spent waiting at the pickup, and POL-NS-22 licenses the fee only where the driver cancels after that wait. No wait was served at the pickup, so the precondition the fee rests on is not met.\n\nWhat does not change the result. The driver met the contact obligation (POL-NS-23): two in-app messages at 08:07:10 and 08:14:00 and one in-app call at 08:15:20, all before the 08:19:10 cancellation. So POL-NS-23 does not independently void the fee, and the 5.00 SGD goodwill credit under POL-NS-24 is not available either, because that credit requires the driver also to have failed to attempt contact, which he did not. No escalation: POL-GEN-26 applies only where a needed fact cannot be established because its evidence is missing or impaired; the GPS trace is complete at weight 1.0 and settles the driver's position, and the only impaired artifact, EV-ADVIS-05057-01 (traffic advisory, status MISSING, issue MISSING_ARTIFACT, weight 0.0), carries no fact that any no-show rule needs. Whether the rider was herself at the pickup is not decisive and is not relied on: the fee fails on the driver's own obligation. POL-GEN-22 and POL-NS-26 put this 5.00 SGD dispute (below the 10.00 SGD band, no fraud flag on either profile) with the AI, final at any confidence; the confidence cap here is 1.0, because the deciding fact is carried by two independent weight-1.0 artifacts.\n\nThe 5.00 SGD is returned to the rider. The driver receives no compensation for a wait not served at the pickup, so driver_compensation_sgd is null, and no clause produces a referral."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "FULL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 5.0,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.95,
    "explanation": "Rider and driver: the 5.00 SGD no-show fee charged on this trip (cancellation_fee 5.00 and total_charged 5.00, EV-FARE-05057) is refunded to the rider in full. No compensation is paid to the driver.\n\nWhy. The booking was for Block 251, Hougang Avenue 3 with a scheduled pickup at 08:10 (EV-TRIP-05057). The driver tapped 'arrived' at 08:06:50 and cancelled at 08:19:10 as a rider no-show. The Tribunal's arrival test (POL-NS-21) asks whether the trip record's arrival event is corroborated by the GPS trace within the arrival radius: 50 m for a STANDARD-class pickup. It is not. The driver-device trace (EV-GPS-05057: 47 fixes at 30 s, 100% coverage, no gaps against the 120 s policy threshold) puts the driver's nearest approach to the booked pickup at 476.59 m, at 08:04:50 (accuracy 8.0 m, inside the 25 m filter), and from 08:06:50 to the 08:19:10 cancellation every fix reads 0 km/h at a stationary point roughly 650 m away. The driver was never at the booked pickup. His own chat message at 08:07:10 ('I reach already at Blk 215 carpark') places him at a different block, and the rider's message at 08:11:30 places her at 'Blk 251'.\n\nThe driver's case therefore fails on POL-NS-24. That clause refunds any fee charged in full where the driver fails to arrive at the pickup by the scheduled pickup time plus five minutes — 08:10 + 5 min = 08:15:00 (driver_late_after_scheduled_sec 300). He had not arrived by 08:15:00 or at any time, so the whole contested 5.00 SGD is returned. His claim resting on arrived_by_deadline = true (EV-TRIP-05057) is rejected: that flag is computed from the driver's own 'arrived' tap, and a status a party sets records only that the party set it. The arrival test requires GPS corroboration, which is absent, so the tap does not establish arrival.\n\nI have considered the driver's other points. The contact obligation in POL-NS-23 was met — two driver messages (08:07:10, 08:14:00) and one unanswered 15 s in-app call (08:15:20), all before the 08:19:10 cancellation — so no refund flows from that clause, and no 5.00 SGD goodwill credit arises under POL-NS-24 (the driver did attempt contact). But contact and location are separate obligations, and the fee cannot stand on a free wait that was never served at the pickup: POL-NS-22 licenses the fee only where the driver cancels after the free wait, and the free wait (POL-NS-21) is a wait at the pickup, which did not happen. The driver's submission that the 50 m radius is a mere evidence procedure that voids no fee is rejected: it is the Tribunal's evidence procedure for whether the driver is at the pickup, and POL-NS-21 and POL-NS-24 read it. His NO_ACTION argument (that the present charge simply stands because nothing voids it) fails because POL-NS-24 positively entitles the rider to a full refund here.\n\nNo escalation is open (POL-GEN-26), as the rider correctly argues. The facts the rules need are established, not missing: the trace is COMPLETE at weight 1.0. The only impaired artifact is EV-ADVIS-05057-01, a traffic advisory whose status is MISSING with issue MISSING_ARTIFACT and weight 0.0; it carries no fact, and no no-show rule needs a traffic record, so its absence leaves no rule element unestablished. The absence of a rider-side GPS track does not open the question either — the deciding fact is where the driver was, and that is measured.\n\nNo referral arises. No goodwill credit (POL-NS-24 contact attempted, above). No repeat no-show flag (POL-NS-25): the rider's profile carries no no_show_cancellations_30d counter, and the fee does not stand. No fee-amount referral (POL-NS-22): the fee charged is exactly the 5.00 SGD the policy fixes. The dispute value is 5.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band, and neither party carries a fraud flag, so under POL-GEN-22 and POL-NS-26 this ruling is final."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}
